      "exercise": "940",
      "answers": {"a": true, "b": false, "c": true, "d": false, "e": true, "f": true},
      "derivations": {
        "a": "Multiplication by positive 5 preserves a strict inequality.",
        "b": "Multiplication by negative -8 reverses the inequality; x1=0,x2=1 is a counterexample.",
        "c": "The real cube function is strictly increasing: x2^3-x1^3=(x2-x1)(x2^2+x1x2+x1^2)>0 when x2>x1.",
        "d": "For positive x2>x1, their reciprocals satisfy 1/x2<1/x1, so the stated greater-than claim is false.",
        "e": "Positive reciprocal differences give 1/x1-1/x2=(x2-x1)/(x1x2)>0.",
        "f": "With x2>x1>0, x2^2-x1^2=(x2-x1)(x2+x1)>0."
      }
    },
    {
      "exercise": "941",
      "answers": {"a": true, "b": true, "c": true, "d": false, "e": true, "f": true},
      "definition_used": "Strict global increase/decrease bound by frozen independent FIRST: alg7-c4-s1-n34 prose[12,14,15], source p0216#2,#4,#5; lesson SHA256 f64b39f92c002a451427bd1f8d3358383d7da597e82508478c4c7a839ba3680f.",
      "derivations": {
        "a": "For strictly increasing f, f(a)>f(b) rules out a<=b, hence a>b.",
        "b": "For strictly decreasing g, g(a)>g(b) forces a<b.",
        "c": "For strictly increasing f, f(a)<f(b) forces a<b.",
        "d": "For strictly decreasing g, g(a)<g(b) forces a>b, not a<b.",
        "e": "For a!=b, f(a)-f(b) and a-b have the same sign, so their quotient is positive.",
        "f": "For a!=b, strict decrease makes g(a)-g(b) and a-b have opposite signs in both argument orders; their quotient is strictly negative, so the source's stated negative quotient is true."
      }
    },
